# Project Standards

- Inspect the existing codebase before making changes. Never assume the app is a blank project.
- Preserve the current framework, package manager, architecture, and working functionality unless a change is justified.
- Prefer small, focused, reversible changes over unnecessary rewrites.
- Reuse existing components, utilities, design tokens, and installed dependencies before creating new ones.
- Do not install dependencies unless needed. Explain the reason before adding a significant dependency.
- Keep components cohesive, typed, accessible, and maintainable. Avoid giant components and duplicated logic.
- Never fabricate asset paths, package APIs, environment variables, or project requirements. Inspect the repository and verify assumptions.
- Treat assets/ as read-only source material unless explicitly instructed otherwise. Never delete, rename, move, or overwrite assets without permission.
- Avoid unrelated refactors and do not overwrite user changes.
- Run relevant lint, typecheck, build, and tests when available. Report checks that could not be run.
- After changes, summarize files changed, behavior affected, checks performed, and known issues.
- Ask a concise question when a missing decision materially affects architecture or visual direction; otherwise make a reasonable, reversible choice.

## Planning Before Implementation

- For major creative, architectural, and technical decisions, collaborate with the user before implementation.
- When the user is discussing ideas, exploring concepts, or planning the project, do not modify files, install dependencies, generate the application, or start implementation until explicitly asked.
- Help define the concept, compare approaches, explain trade-offs, identify risks, and maintain an agreed implementation plan.
- Ask focused questions when important decisions remain unresolved. Avoid asking about details that can be decided later.
- Distinguish between brainstorming, planning, and implementation requests.
- Once implementation is explicitly authorized, work in small, verifiable stages.
- Before large architectural changes or destructive operations, explain the intended change and obtain approval.